---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../consts';

// NOTE FOR THE DEVELOPER (not shown on the page): this policy is written to be
// accurate and honest, but it is not legal advice. Before you rely on it for the
// App Store or for EU/UK traffic, have a real lawyer read it. See COMPLIANCE.md.
// Keep this in sync with what the app ACTUALLY does: Firebase Auth (Google +
// email/password), Firestore for account + game data, Firebase Analytics, and
// the admin dashboard. Photos are still device-only (not uploaded).

const title = 'Privacy Policy | Questling';
const description =
  'What Questling collects and stores. Using the app needs a free account, and your account and game progress are saved in the cloud. Your Memory Journal photos stay on your device. Nothing is sold or shared.';

// The date the policy was last actually changed (not auto-updated).
const lastUpdated = 'July 24, 2026';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="updated">Last updated {lastUpdated}</p>

      <p class="lead">
        I'm the developer of Questling. This policy explains, in plain language, what the
        Questling app and this website (getquestling.com) collect, where it is stored, and
        who can see it. Using the app now needs a free account, so there is real information
        involved, and I want to be straight with you about all of it.
      </p>

      <h2>You need a free account</h2>
      <p>
        To use the app you sign in, either with Google or with an email and password. That
        is how your quests, gems, and rewards stay saved and follow you to another device.
        When you sign in, I collect and store:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>your <strong>email address</strong></li>
        <li>your <strong>name</strong>, if you sign in with Google (Google shares the name on your account)</li>
        <li>a <strong>user ID</strong> that the sign-in system assigns to your account</li>
        <li>a <strong>username</strong> (it starts as your Google name and you can edit it), plus the dates your account was created and last used</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Your game progress is saved in the cloud</h2>
      <p>
        So your progress isn't stuck on one device, the app saves your game data to the
        cloud while you're signed in:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>your gems, the quests you've finished, and the quest you're on now</li>
        <li>the rewards you set up in the Shop</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is stored using Google Firebase (see below). It is tied to your account, not
        sold or shared, and used only to run the app for you.
      </p>

      <h2>Your photos stay on your phone</h2>
      <p>
        The Memory Journal is the exception, on purpose. Any photos you add to a quest are
        saved <strong>only on your own device</strong> and are never uploaded to the cloud
        or to me. They do not sync between devices, and I never see them. Deleting a photo
        in the app, or deleting the app, removes them.
      </p>

      <h2>Who this data is stored with (Google Firebase)</h2>
      <p>
        Sign-in, accounts, and the cloud save all run on <strong>Google Firebase</strong>
        (Firebase Authentication and Cloud Firestore). That means your account details and
        game data are held on Google's servers, which may be located in the United States,
        and are protected by Google's security. Firebase also sends the password-reset email
        if you ask for one. If you sign in with Google, Google's own privacy policy also
        applies to that sign-in.
      </p>

      <h2>Who can see your data</h2>
      <p>
        You can, in the app. And I can: I have an admin tool that lets me see account
        details (username, email, sign-up and last-seen dates) and game stats (gems and
        quests completed), so I can help with support questions, fix a problem, and keep the
        app running. I do <strong>not</strong> sell, rent, or share your personal
        information with anyone, and I don't use it for advertising.
      </p>

      <h2>Analytics</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>In the app.</strong> I use Firebase Analytics to understand roughly how the
          app is used (which screens people visit, whether features work), in aggregate. It
          helps me improve the app and is not used to advertise to you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>On this website.</strong> I use Cloudflare Web Analytics for rough traffic
          numbers (page views, referrers, countries). It is cookieless, stores nothing on
          your device, and cannot identify you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fonts.</strong> The site loads the Inter typeface from Google Fonts, so
          Google may receive your IP address to deliver the font. That is standard for Google
          Fonts.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Cookies and local storage</h2>
      <p>
        The marketing website sets no cookies and the analytics on it is cookieless, so you
        won't see a consent banner there. The app itself keeps your sign-in session in your
        browser's local storage so you stay logged in between visits. That is not an
        advertising cookie; it's the standard way signing in works, and clearing your browser
        data or signing out removes it.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        Questling is made for a parent to run on their own phone. The account belongs to the
        parent (or another adult), not the child: children do not create accounts, do not
        sign in, and do not enter any information themselves. The only personal details I hold
        are the account holder's (an adult's) email and name. I do not knowingly collect
        personal information from a child under 13. This is how I try to stay in step with
        children's privacy rules like COPPA. If you're a parent with a question about any of
        this, please email me and I'll answer honestly.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <span class="badge"><span class="badge__dot"></span>Coming soon</span>
        <h2>Subscriptions</h2>
        <p>
          Questling is free today, with every quest unlocked. I may add an optional paid
          subscription later, handled through Apple's in-app purchase system (likely using a
          service called RevenueCat to manage it). If that goes live:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Apple processes the payment. I never see your card number or billing details.
          </li>
          <li>
            Apple and RevenueCat may handle a small amount of purchase and
            anonymous-identifier data needed to run the subscription, such as whether a
            purchase is currently active.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This section is a placeholder, and none of it is active yet. When a subscription
          launches, I'll update this policy with the specifics and change the date at the top
          before it turns on.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
      <p>
        Photos in the Memory Journal live only on your device, so deleting a photo in the
        app, or deleting the app, removes them. To delete your <strong>account and the cloud
        data tied to it</strong> (your profile and game progress), email me at
        <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> and I'll delete it for you.
        You can also sign out any time from the Account screen.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If I change this policy, I'll update the "Last updated" date at the top. If a change
        is significant, I'll make it clear rather than quiet.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Email me at <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. A real person,
        me, reads it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .container--narrow { max-width: 720px; }
  h1 { margin-bottom: 0.25rem; }
  .updated { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .lead { font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--ink); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.3rem; margin-top: 2.2rem; }
  p, li { color: var(--ink); }
  ul { margin: 0 0 1.1rem; padding-left: 1.3rem; }
  li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  a { color: var(--primary); text-decoration: underline; }

  /* The subscription notice is a full-bordered callout, not a live section, so it
     reads as "not active yet" at a glance. */
  .callout {
    margin-top: 2.4rem;
    padding: 1.4rem 1.6rem;
    background: var(--primary-tint);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .callout h2 { margin-top: 0.7rem; }
  .callout p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
</style>
